# Why buy both insurance and lottery tickets?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What is risk aversion? How can the same person be cautious and a gambler?"
next: {"title":"Is losing worse than winning is good?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/86-is-losing-worse-than-winning-is-good.cast"}

## Two losing bets
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Roulette (one zero)
      - US lottery
    values:
      - 97
      - 67
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: ¢
      decimals: 0
    bar_colors: each
    colors:
      - purple
      - orange
    icons:
      - roulette
      - ticket
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 110
    y_label: Paid back per $1 bet, cents
    label_size: 28
    reference:
      value: 100
      label: break even
      color: grey
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 110
      w: 600
      h: 540
sources:
    - id: census
      title: Sales of Lottery Tickets Nearly Doubled and Prizes More Than Doubled From 2008 to 2024
      authors: U.S. Census Bureau
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Annual Survey of State Government Finances: state lottery ticket sales $104.7 billion and prizes $70.2 billion in fiscal 2024 (about 67 cents in prizes per dollar of sales).'
      url: https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2026/04/state-lottery-ticket-sales-soar.html
    - id: gallup
      title: Gambling in America (Gallup poll, June–July 2026)
      authors: Gallup
      year: 2026
      finding: 31% of US adults said they had bought a state lottery ticket in the past 12 months (49% in 2016).

    box ins "Insurance:\nless than $1 back" x 830 y 560 width 300 height 150 radius 12 icon umbrella font_size 32 hidden true
    column kinds gap 24 fit.h 200 fit.w 280 fit.x 690 fit.y 250 hidden true
    box k1 "careful → insure" #2f6b8f width 280 height 80 radius 10 font_size 28 in kinds hidden true
    box k2 "reckless → gamble" #b5482e width 280 height 80 radius 10 font_size 28 in kinds hidden true
    mark x1 around k1 #b5482e kind strike hidden true
    mark x2 around k2 #b5482e kind strike hidden true
    text both "3 in 10 adults\nbought a ticket" #3d3833 cites ["gallup"] x 830 y 170 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Two losing bets"

Why do so many people buy insurance and lottery tickets, sometimes in the same week?
    draw axes reference_1

B: Both are bets that lose money on average. On a roul (@draw bar_1@)ette wheel with a single zero, each dollar bet comes back as about 97 cents.

    ask "Drag the lottery bar to the cents you think come back per dollar." on bar_2 store g default "40"
        intro: Now American state lotteries. Of every dollar spent on tickets, how much comes back as prizes?
        right: Close: you said {g} cents, and it's {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g} cents; it's {g.true}.

B: About 67 cents. In 2024 Americans spent nearly 105 billion dollars on state lottery tickets and won back about 70 billion.
    highlight bar_2

Insurance is the same bet turned round. The insurer pays out less in  (@highlight reference_1@)claims than it collects, or it couldn't pay its staff and its owners.
    draw ins

B: The easy story is two kinds of people: careful ones insure, reckless ones gamble.
    draw k1 k2

But the (@draw x1 x2@)y overlap. Three in ten American adu (@draw both@)lts bought a lottery ticket last year, and car insurance alone is compulsory in nearly every state.

    erase ins

B: So the same person pays to get rid of a risk on Monday, and pays to take one on Saturday.
    highlight bar_2 reference_1

    erase k1 k2 x1 x2 both

To make sense of that, we need a way to put a price on risk itself, not just on its average.
    text q "price of risk?" #8a5fa8 x 830 y 400 font_size 54

    pause 0.5

## Bend the curve
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 112
    'y':
      - 0
      - 11
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 195
      w: 650
      h: 465
vars:
    bend:
      value: 0.5
      min: 0.05
      max: 1.8
      step: 0.05
    UL:
      expr: 10*(0.16)^bend
    EU:
      expr: 0.5*UL + 5
    ce:
      expr: 100*(EU/10)^(1/bend)
    rp:
      expr: 58 - ce
sources:
    - id: holt
      title: Risk Aversion and Incentive Effects
      authors: Holt, C. A. & Laury, S. K.
      year: 2002
      finding: 'In paired lottery choices most subjects were risk averse, and more so when the stakes were scaled up (American Economic Review 92(5): 1644–1655).'

    axes ax y_label "utility U" cross corner ticks.x [16,58,100] hidden true
    text xl "wealth, $ thousand" x 360 y 158 font_size 26 hidden true
    dot oL #b5482e at.x 16 at.y 0 hidden true
    label l_oL "storm" above #b5482e attach_to oL hidden true
    dot oH #2f6b8f at.x 100 at.y 0 hidden true
    label l_oH "good season" above #2f6b8f attach_to oH hidden true
    dot oM #3d3833 at.x 58 at.y 0 hidden true
    label l_oM "average" above attach_to oM hidden true
    curve u #2f6b8f expr 10*(x/100)^bend x_from 0 x_to 104 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text bern "each extra $\nadds less" #2f6b8f x 850 y 560 font_size 32 hidden true
    label l_conc "concave" above #2f6b8f attach_to u hidden true
    dot L #b5482e at.on u at.x 16 hidden true
    dot H #2f6b8f at.on u at.x 100 hidden true
    curve ch dashed thick #8f887c x_expr "16 + 84*t" y_expr "UL + (10 - UL)*t" t_from 0 t_to 1 hidden true
    dot M #8a5fa8 at.x 58 at.y 7 bind {"at.y":"EU"} hidden true
    label l_M "expected utility" below #8a5fa8 attach_to M hidden true
    dot C #3d3833 at.x 49 at.y 7 bind {"at.x":"ce","at.y":"EU"} hidden true
    label l_C "sure ${ce:0}k" left attach_to C font_size 28 hidden true
    curve rpseg #b5482e x_expr "ce + (58 - ce)*t" y_expr EU t_from 0 t_to 1 style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    text ro "average  $58k\nsure thing  ${ce:0}k\nrisk premium  ${rp:0}k" #3d3833 x 850 y 470 font_size 32 hidden true
    text lover "risk lover" #b5482e x 850 y 330 font_size 40 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mcard work [{"tex":"EU = \\tfrac12\\sqrt{16} + \\tfrac12\\sqrt{100} = 7"},{"tex":"CE = 7^2 = 49"},{"tex":"\\text{premium} = 58 - 49 = 9"}] x 810 y 290 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Bend the curve"

B: Both bets lose on average, so people must weigh something besides the average. Take a Harbourtown fisher, Nils, with made-up numbers.
    draw ax xl

B: His season is a co (@draw oH l_oH@)in toss: a good one leaves him with $100,000 (@draw oL l_oL@), a storm-wrecked one with just $16,000. On (@draw oM l_oM@) average, $58,000.

    quiz "What is the smallest sure sum you'd take instead of Nils's coin toss?" judge false voice b
        right: Anything below $58,000 means you'd give up money to be rid of the risk, and most people in experiments do.
        * $58,000 or more
        * $45,000 to $57,000
        * Below $45,000

I'd take a sure fifty thousand myself. So what's going on in my head?
    point at.x 50 at.y 0 gesture tap

B: A curve like Daniel Bernoulli's, from the St Petersburg game: count utility, no (@draw bern@)t dollars, and each extra dollar adds a little less.
    draw u

B: So the curve bends: steep when you're poor, flatter as you get richer. (@draw l_conc@) That shape is called concave.

    erase bern

Where does Nils's coin toss sit on it?
    highlight oL oH

    erase l_conc l_oL l_oH l_oM oL oH oM

B: One point for each outcome. Join them, a (@draw ch@)nd halfway along is his expe (@draw M l_M@)cted utility: the average of the two heights.
    draw L H

B: Now go left until the c (@draw C l_C@)urve reaches that same height. A sure $49,000 gives him just as much utility as the gamble.

So the gamble is worth $49,000 to him, not $58,000.
    highlight C M

B: That's his certainty equivalent. The $9,000  (@draw rpseg@)gap is the risk premiu (@draw ro@)m: what he'd pay just to be rid of the risk.

Which is exactly the room an insurer needs: charge a bit more than the storm's average cost, and both sides still gain.
    highlight rpseg

    erase l_M

B: Your turn: drag the curve. Straighten it, then bend it hard, and watch the risk premium.
    explore params ["bend"]

Straight, and the premium is zero: he only cares about the ave (@animate bend 0.1 duration 2.5@)rage. Bend it hard, and it climbs to about $16,000.
    animate bend 1 duration 2

B: Bend it upward, and the premium turns negative: he'd pay to keep the gamble. T (@draw lover@)hat's a risk lover.
    animate bend 1.6 duration 3

    erase lover

So risk aversion is just a bent curve: the more it bends, the more he'd give up to swap the gamble for a sure thing.
    animate bend 0.5 duration 2.5

    pause 0.4

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own store mm default "Skip it"

@math

    erase mathq

    fade ax xl u ch L H M C l_C rpseg to 0.15

Expected utility is half of root 16 plus half of root 100: 2 plus 5, which is 7.
    draw mcard_box mcard_line_1

Which sure sum gives utility 7? Seven squared: $49,000.
    draw mcard_line_2

And 58 minus 49 leaves the $9,000 risk premium.
    draw mcard_line_3

    erase mcard

@test

    erase mathq

    fade ax xl u ch L H M C l_C rpseg to 1

    pause 0.3

## One curve, two bends
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Friedman and Savage would sign that.
    good: Your curve bends the right way.
    poor: 'A flat curve: risk doesn''t seem to scare you.'
    none: 'No bets placed: very risk averse.'
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 105
    'y':
      - 0
      - 10
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 195
      w: 620
      h: 465
vars:
    U10:
      expr: 3*(1-exp(-10/15)) + 6/(1+exp(-(10-55)/7))
    U25:
      expr: 3*(1-exp(-25/15)) + 6/(1+exp(-(25-55)/7))
    U95:
      expr: 3*(1-exp(-95/15)) + 6/(1+exp(-(95-55)/7))
sources:
    - id: fs
      title: The Utility Analysis of Choices Involving Risk
      authors: Friedman, M. & Savage, L. J.
      year: 1948
      finding: 'A utility-of-wealth curve concave at low wealth, convex in a middle stretch and concave again at the top can explain why the same people buy insurance and lottery tickets; the segments are read as different socioeconomic levels (Journal of Political Economy 56(4): 279–304).'
      doi: 10.1086/256692
    - id: mark
      title: The Utility of Wealth
      authors: Markowitz, H.
      year: 1952
      finding: 'Criticises the Friedman–Savage curve''s implications (e.g. that those at the top would never take risks, and only the middle would gamble) and places the curve''s bends at each person''s customary wealth, measuring gains and losses from there (Journal of Political Economy 60(2): 151–158).'

    axes ax y_label "utility U" cross corner hidden true
    text xl "wealth" x 340 y 158 font_size 28 hidden true
    polygon band points [[28,0],[55,0],[55,10],[28,10]] data true style.fill #c96567 style.opacity 0.12 style.stroke_width 0 hidden true
    curve fs #2f6b8f expr "3*(1-exp(-x/15)) + 6/(1+exp(-(x-55)/7))" x_from 0 x_to 100 cites ["fs"] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    portrait fried x 840 y 500 width 190 of "Milton Friedman" cameo true hidden true
    text c1 "cautious" #2f6b8f at.data [11,3.4] font_size 30 hidden true
    text g "gambler" #b5482e at.data [33,6.2] font_size 30 hidden true
    text c2 "cautious" #2f6b8f at.data [80,9.6] font_size 30 hidden true
    dot N #3d3833 at.on fs at.x 25 hidden true
    label l_N "Nils" below attach_to N font_size 30 hidden true
    dot S #b5482e at.on fs at.x 10 hidden true
    label l_S "storm" right #b5482e attach_to S hidden true
    curve chS dashed thick #b5482e x_expr "10 + 15*t" y_expr "U10 + (U25 - U10)*t" t_from 0 t_to 1 hidden true
    dot J #2f6b8f at.on fs at.x 95 hidden true
    label l_J "jackpot" below #2f6b8f attach_to J hidden true
    curve NJ dashed thick #8a5fa8 x_expr "25 + 70*t" y_expr "U25 + (U95 - U25)*t" t_from 0 t_to 1 hidden true
    text tick "ticket worth\nmore than\nits price" #8a5fa8 x 840 y 420 font_size 34 hidden true
    text mark "Harry Markowitz\n1952" #3d3833 cites ["mark"] x 840 y 520 font_size 34 hidden true
    dot R #3d3833 at.on fs at.x 82 hidden true
    label l_R "rich" below attach_to R font_size 30 hidden true
    text mid "only the middle\ngambles?" #b5482e cites ["mark"] x 840 y 300 font_size 34 hidden true
    text own "bulge at your\nown wealth" #3d3833 cites ["mark"] x 830 y 300 font_size 34 hidden true
    text fun "or: the fun\nof the dream" #8a5fa8 x 840 y 300 font_size 34 hidden true
    text st1 "big-stakes risk aversion:\nmostly agreed" #3d3833 x 820 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    text st2 "why both:\ncontested" #b5482e x 820 y 430 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "One curve, two bends"

A bent curve explains the insurance. But it makes the lottery ticket stranger still: someone that cautious should never take a losing bet.
    draw ax xl

B: Unless the curve bends both ways. In 1 (@draw fried@)948 Milton Frie (@draw fs@)dman and Leonard Savage drew one that does.

It be (@draw c1@)nds cautiously at low wealth, tur (@draw g@)ns upward in the middle, and flattens  (@draw c2@)again near the top.

    erase fried

B: They read the upward stretch as a jump between classes: money that would change your whole way of life, not just add to it.
    draw band

Put Nils just below that bulge. A storm would  (@draw S l_S chS@)drop him down the cautious stretch, so he still insures.
    draw N l_N

B: And a lottery ticket?
    draw J l_J

    quiz "Near Nils, does the line from him to the jackpot run above his curve or below it?" judge false voice b
        right: Let's draw it.
        * Above the curve
        * Below the curve

Near Nils, the line to the jackpot runs above his curve. A few dollars c (@draw tick@)ost him almost nothing, while the jackpot would lift him over the bulge.
    draw NJ

    erase S l_S chS NJ J l_J tick c1 g c2

B: But Harry Markowitz, then a young Chicago economist, saw a problem in 1952.
    draw mark

    erase mark

The bulge sits at the same wealth for everyone. Then o (@draw mid@)nly people in the middle would gamble, and the  (@draw R l_R@)very rich would never take a risk.
    highlight band

    erase mid

B: Yet the rich gamble too. His fix: put t (@draw own@)he bulge wherever each person already stands, so what counts is gains and losses from there.

    erase own

Others say a ticket buys something else entirely: a week of daydreaming about the jackpot.
    draw fun

    erase fun R l_R

B: How sure are we? That most people are risk a (@draw st1@)verse when the stakes are large is mostly agreed. Why the same pe (@draw st2@)ople also gamble is still contested.

    quiz "A store offers a $4 extended warranty on a $15 toaster. On the bent-curve logic, who should buy it?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Almost nobody: over $15 any curve is nearly straight, so the risk premium is pennies, far below the warranty's mark-up.
        wrong: How much does a curve bend over a $15 stretch? Barely: almost nobody.
        + Almost nobody
        * Anyone risk averse
        * Only the poorest

    quiz "Two fishers face the same storm risk; one has $1 million saved, the other $40,000. With a √ curve like Nils's, who pays more for the same storm cover?" voice b
        right: The one with $40,000: the curve bends hardest at low wealth, so the same loss hurts him more.
        wrong: Where is the curve most bent, low or high? Low: the one with $40,000.
        + The one with $40,000
        * The one with $1 million
        * Both the same

    quiz "Two lotteries pay out the same on average: one has many $5 prizes, the other a single $10 million jackpot. Which one tempts a Friedman–Savage gambler?"
        right: The jackpot: only a prize big enough to carry him over the bulge is worth more to him than its price.
        wrong: Which prize could carry him over the bulge? Only the big one: the jackpot.
        + The jackpot
        * The $5 prizes
        * Neither

So why buy both? A sure thing beats a gamble with the same average when a loss would hurt most,
    point at.ref N gesture circle

and a long shot can be worth more than its price when it could change your life.
    highlight band

And where you stand when you choose turns out to matter a lot.
    highlight fs

    pause 0.5

